<div class="page-wrap prose">
  <section id="question" aria-labelledby="question-title">
    <h2 id="question-title">Taking Kafka away changes less than you think</h2>
    <p>
      Kafka gives a CDC pipeline a replayable log, partitions that keep a key in order, a place to store connector
      offsets and, with an opt-in, a documented exactly-once hop. Run Debezium without it and you lose those, but the
      delivery guarantee does not get better: it is <a href="{{ '/glossary/#at-least-once' | url }}">at-least-once</a>,
      duplicates follow every crash, and the destination still has to decide which version of a row wins. End-to-end
      exactly-once across independent systems is not achievable in general. What you can reach is
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#effectively-once' | url }}">effectively-once</a> processing: an
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#idempotent-write' | url }}">idempotent write</a> keyed on the primary key and ordered by
      the <a href="{{ '/glossary/#lsn-scn' | url }}">source log position</a>, with deletes kept as markers.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page covers four non-Kafka paths: <a href="#server">Debezium Server</a>, the
      <a href="#engine">embedded engine</a>, <a href="#managed">managed CDC services</a>, and a consumer you write
      yourself that keeps a <a href="#search">search index or cache</a> in sync. For the Kafka-side claims see
      <a href="{{ '/is-cdc-exactly-once/' | url }}">Is CDC exactly-once?</a>; for the per-target ordering rules see
      <a href="{{ '/which-row-wins/' | url }}">Which row wins</a>. Transport semantics (Pulsar, Kinesis, Pub/Sub) are in
      <a href="{{ '/event-envelope/#transports' | url }}">Transports other than Kafka</a>; the tool landscape is in
      <a href="{{ '/tooling/' | url }}">Tooling</a> and <a href="{{ '/compare/' | url }}">Compare</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>As of 2026-10-09.</strong> Read against the Debezium 3.7 documentation. The Elasticsearch and Redis
      behaviour in the <a href="#search">search section</a> was run on Elasticsearch 9.5.5 and Redis 8.10.2, single
      local nodes. Nothing else on this page was run against a live product, and where a fact comes from source code
      it says so and says &ldquo;check your version&rdquo;.
    </p>
  </section>

