  <section id="server" aria-labelledby="server-title">
    <h2 id="server-title">Debezium Server: one connector, one sink, offsets in a store you pick</h2>
    <p>
      Debezium&rsquo;s documentation describes Debezium Server as a ready-to-use application that streams change events
      from a source database directly to messaging and storage systems without requiring Apache Kafka. Each instance
      runs exactly one connector. You choose the destination with <code>debezium.sink.type</code>, prefix connector
      settings with <code>debezium.source.</code>, and set the offset store yourself, because the Kafka Connect runtime
      that normally does it is not there. The excerpt below is the documentation&rsquo;s own Kinesis example (not run by
      this site):
    </p>
<pre><code>debezium.sink.type=kinesis
debezium.sink.kinesis.region=eu-central-1
debezium.source.connector.class=io.debezium.connector.postgresql.PostgresConnector
debezium.source.offset.storage.file.filename=data/offsets.dat
debezium.source.offset.flush.interval.ms=0
debezium.source.database.hostname=localhost
debezium.source.topic.prefix=tutorial</code></pre>
    <h3>Where the offset lives, and what a crash does</h3>
    <p>
      Debezium&rsquo;s state-storage page says that if you run a connector in Debezium Server or the embedded engine you
      must configure the offset store explicitly. The options it documents:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>File</strong> (<code>FileOffsetBackingStore</code>, the Server default): a local file; the page calls it ideal for single-node applications or testing.</li>
      <li><strong>JDBC</strong> (<code>JdbcOffsetBackingStore</code>): a table, <code>debezium_offset_storage</code> by default, in the source database or another one.</li>
      <li><strong>Redis</strong> (<code>RedisOffsetBackingStore</code>): a hash key, <code>metadata:debezium:offsets</code> by default on the Server page.</li>
      <li><strong>Kafka</strong> (<code>KafkaOffsetBackingStore</code>): a compacted topic. It is documented on the storage page; the Server page lists only file, memory, JDBC and Redis, so check that your Server version accepts it.</li>
      <li><strong>Memory</strong>: volatile, lost on a crash, for tests only.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In every case the store is written by the engine on its own schedule. Nothing in these pages puts the offset in the
      same transaction as your destination write. The embedded engine documentation states the consequence for a
      crash: the application &ldquo;will likely receive some source records after restart that it had already
      processed&rdquo;, up to <em>n</em> duplicates with a flush interval of 0 (<em>n</em> being the batch size) and up to
      <em>n &times; m</em> with a longer interval. Set <code>offset.flush.interval.ms</code> explicitly: the engine page
      says its default is one minute while the storage page lists 6000 ms, so check your version.
    </p>
    <h3>Per-sink facts that matter for duplicates and order</h3>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="Debezium Server sinks" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>What the Debezium Server page and each destination&rsquo;s documentation say (read 2026-10-09)</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Sink</th>
          <th scope="col">What is stated</th>
          <th scope="col">What it means for the consumer</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Kinesis</th>
          <td>The Kinesis documentation says producer retries can store a record twice with different sequence numbers, and that applications needing strict guarantees should embed a primary key to remove duplicates. AWS DMS&rsquo;s page says Kinesis Data Streams does not support deduplication. <code>PutRecords</code> &ldquo;doesn&rsquo;t guarantee the ordering of records&rdquo; because one failed record does not stop the rest.</td>
          <td>Never rely on arrival order. In the sink source on <code>main</code> (may differ from your release) failed entries of a batch are retried in a later request, which can put them after later changes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Google Pub/Sub</th>
          <td>Debezium: <code>ordering.enabled</code> defaults to true and uses the message key. Google: Pub/Sub delivers each message at least once; ordering is per ordering key, and a redelivery re-delivers later messages for that key. Exactly-once delivery is a pull-subscription feature based on a Pub/Sub-assigned message ID.</td>
          <td>My reading, not stated by either page: a change re-published after a Server crash gets a new message ID, so subscription-side exactly-once would not recognise it as a duplicate.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Apache Pulsar</th>
          <td>Pulsar deduplication is off by default, enabled per broker, namespace or topic, and uses the producer name to filter duplicates. Debezium passes <code>debezium.sink.pulsar.producer.*</code> through to the producer.</td>
<td>I did not verify how the sink names its producer or whether a restarted Server presents the same identity, so do not count on broker deduplication across a Server restart. Check your version.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Redis Streams</th>
          <td>The sink writes with <code>XADD</code>; its client interface takes no entry ID (source on <code>main</code>), and Redis documents <code>*</code> as auto-generating a unique ID.</td>
          <td>A re-sent change is a second stream entry. The reader dedupes on the position in the payload.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP client</th>
          <td>Retries 5 times by default, then raises an error; optional batching into a JSON array.</td>
          <td>A retry after a lost response posts the same event again: the receiver must be idempotent.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">JDBC</th>
          <td>The page says Debezium Server &ldquo;does not currently provide the same delivery guarantees&rdquo; as the connector on Kafka Connect, naming offset management, exactly-once and retries as features that may behave differently.</td>
          <td>Do not carry over assumptions from the <a href="{{ '/is-cdc-exactly-once/#sink' | url }}">Kafka Connect JDBC sink</a>. Keep <code>insert.mode=upsert</code> and add your own position guard.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Iceberg</th>
          <td>Community sink in a separate repository. Its deduplication is per batch only and <code>upsert-dedup-column</code> has no default; the full finding is on <a href="{{ '/which-row-wins/#iceberg' | url }}">Which row wins</a>.</td>
          <td>No protection across batches: guard with a <code>MERGE</code> on the position.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Databricks Zerobus</th>
          <td>&ldquo;Delivery uses an at-least-once guarantee. As a result, the sink might deliver the same record more than once.&rdquo; The page tells you to deduplicate, for example by the source LSN, and offers <code>idempotency.mode</code>.</td>
          <td>This is the thesis in Debezium&rsquo;s own words: deduplicate on the source position.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Debezium&rsquo;s Kafka Connect exactly-once opt-in is documented for connectors deployed in Kafka Connect. These
      pages do not document an equivalent for Debezium Server. There is no Elasticsearch or OpenSearch sink in the
      Server sink list I read, so the search path below is a consumer you write.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="engine" aria-labelledby="engine-title">
    <h2 id="engine-title">The embedded engine: you own the commit</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is a library (<code>debezium-api</code> plus <code>debezium-embedded</code>) that runs a connector
      inside your application. From Debezium 3.2 the default implementation is the asynchronous engine, which Debezium
      Server also uses. When you need batching or an async API, the documentation points to
      <code>DebeziumEngine.ChangeConsumer</code>, whose single method is
      <code>handleBatch(List&lt;R&gt; records, RecordCommitter&lt;R&gt; committer)</code>. Per its Javadoc you call
      <code>markProcessed</code> for each record and <code>markBatchFinished</code> when the batch is done.
    </p>
    <h3>The commit-after-process contract</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Write the record&rsquo;s effect to your destination.</li>
      <li>Only then call <code>markProcessed(record)</code>. It registers that record&rsquo;s source offset to be flushed; in the engine source on <code>main</code> it does that and <code>markBatchFinished</code> asks the commit policy whether to flush now.</li>
      <li>Call <code>markBatchFinished()</code> at the end of the batch.</li>
      <li>If a write fails, do not mark that record or any later one, and stop the engine. My reading, untested: the connector&rsquo;s in-memory position has already moved on, so only a restart re-reads from the last stored offset.</li>
      <li>Stop the engine with <code>close()</code>, not by shutting down the executor. The documentation says an interrupt can leave it unclean and the restarted application may see records it had already processed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Marking a record <em>before</em> its write is durable turns a crash into data loss, because the stored offset has
      moved past a change the destination never got. Marking <em>after</em> turns a crash into a repeat, which an
      idempotent write absorbs. The simple <code>notifying(record -&gt; ...)</code> form has a documented hazard: the page
      says the handler should not throw, and that an exception is logged and the engine moves on to the next record, so
      your application might become inconsistent with the database. The default wrapper in the source on
      <code>main</code> appears to propagate other exceptions instead, so docs and code may disagree; I did not run
      either. Catch inside the handler and decide.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">// Untested sketch. Types follow the engine documentation.
class IdempotentSink implements DebeziumEngine.ChangeConsumer&lt;ChangeEvent&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; {
  @Override
  public void handleBatch(List&lt;ChangeEvent&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; records,
      RecordCommitter&lt;ChangeEvent&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; committer) throws InterruptedException {
    for (ChangeEvent&lt;String, String&gt; r : records) {
      applyIfNewer(r);               // upsert on the key, only if source position is higher
      committer.markProcessed(r);    // after the effect is durable, never before
    }
    committer.markBatchFinished();
  }
}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The documentation also says: &ldquo;applications will receive each source record exactly once during normal operation
      (including restart after a graceful shutdown), but do need to be tolerant of receiving duplicate events
      immediately following a restart after a crash&rdquo;. It then says applications needing more rigorous
      exactly-once behaviour should use &ldquo;the full Debezium platform&rdquo;. Read that against the Debezium
      exactly-once page, which documents only an opt-in with caveats for connectors on Kafka Connect. For the engine the
      safe design is the first sentence: expect duplicates after a crash and make the write idempotent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="managed" aria-labelledby="managed-title">
    <h2 id="managed-title">Managed CDC services: only what their pages state</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="Managed services" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>Delivery and ordering statements found in each service&rsquo;s documentation (read 2026-10-09)</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Service</th>
          <th scope="col">Delivery</th>
          <th scope="col">Ordering and position</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Google Cloud Datastream</th>
          <td>Data is &ldquo;delivered to the destination at least once. No event is missed, but there&rsquo;s a possibility of duplicate events in the stream&rdquo;; the duplicate window &ldquo;should be on the order of minutes&rdquo; and the event UUID detects duplicates.</td>
          <td>&ldquo;Doesn&rsquo;t guarantee ordering&rdquo;. In BigQuery, out-of-order events are merged in sequence using event metadata and an internal change sequence number. For PostgreSQL the page says order can be inferred from <code>source_timestamp</code> together with <code>lsn</code>; use the <code>lsn</code> as your guard.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">AWS DMS</th>
          <td>The pages read state no overall guarantee. They do say Kinesis Data Streams does not support deduplication, so consumers must handle duplicates, and that the S3 target may write duplicates during full load in two cases.</td>
          <td>By default CDC row changes on S3 are stored per table &ldquo;without regard to transaction order&rdquo;; transaction order needs an endpoint setting (<code>PreserveTransactions</code>, CSV only).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Airbyte</th>
          <td>Incremental sync documentation: &ldquo;Airbyte provides an at-least-once delivery guarantee&rdquo;. The CDC page states no delivery guarantee of its own.</td>
          <td>CDC records carry <code>_ab_cdc_lsn</code> (or log file and position for MySQL), <code>_ab_cdc_updated_at</code> and <code>_ab_cdc_deleted_at</code>. The deduped mode sorts by a cursor field; the pages do not say which column that is for CDC, so check your destination connector.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Fivetran</th>
          <td>The core-concepts and PostgreSQL connector pages read state no delivery guarantee.</td>
          <td>Deletes appear as <code>_fivetran_deleted = TRUE</code> on the destination row. No ordering statement was found in the pages read.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Absence from a page is not a guarantee either way; ask the vendor, in writing, which position orders the
      destination and what happens on a restart. If you run your own merge on top of a landing table, the rule from
      <a href="{{ '/which-row-wins/' | url }}">Which row wins</a> applies unchanged. The vendor labs are
      <a href="{{ '/cloud-labs/aws-dms/' | url }}">AWS DMS</a>, <a href="{{ '/cloud-labs/fivetran/' | url }}">Fivetran</a>
      and <a href="{{ '/cloud-labs/snowflake-cdc/' | url }}">Snowflake</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="search" aria-labelledby="search-title">
    <h2 id="search-title">Syncing a search index or cache: the log position is the version</h2>
    <p>
      Elasticsearch&rsquo;s own index API documentation makes the point this site makes: with external versioning
      &ldquo;there is no need to maintain strict ordering of async indexing operations run as a result of changes to a
      source database, as long as version numbers from the source database are used&rdquo;. AWS says the same about
      Kinesis consumers: with OpenSearch &ldquo;you can use a combination of versioning and unique IDs to prevent
      duplicated processing&rdquo;. The recipe: document ID = primary key, document body = the full <code>after</code>
      image, <code>version</code> = the source log position, <code>version_type=external</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>What was run on Elasticsearch 9.5.5</h3>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="Elasticsearch probe results" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>Probe on a single local node, security off, 2026-10-09. HTTP status in brackets.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Request</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
          <th scope="col">Reading</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">index v100, then v200</th><td>created (201), updated (200)</td><td>Higher version wins.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">replay v100 after v200</th><td>version_conflict_engine_exception (409)</td><td>A stale replay is refused.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">replay v200 after v200</th><td>409</td><td>A duplicate is refused too: strictly higher is required. Treat 409 as success.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">delete v300 (<code>DELETE ...?version=300&amp;version_type=external</code>)</th><td>deleted (200), later GET 404</td><td>A delete takes a version like any write.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">late v200 and v100 after the delete</th><td>409 and 409</td><td>The delete is remembered, for now.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">same replay with default <code>index.gc_deletes</code></th><td>409 at every check up to 56 s; accepted (201) at 61 s</td><td>The delete&rsquo;s version was forgotten after about 60 s and the document came back.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">same replay with <code>index.gc_deletes</code> = 2s</th><td>409 at 1 s; accepted at 6 s</td><td>The setting is the window.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">same replay with <code>index.gc_deletes</code> = 10m</th><td>409 at 61 s</td><td>A longer window holds.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">marker document: index v300 with <code>deleted: true</code>, then late v200</th><td>409; a filtered search returns no hit</td><td>A live document has no expiry; nothing resurrects.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">version 0; 2<sup>63</sup>&minus;1; 2<sup>63</sup>; &minus;1; 1.5; <code>16/B374D848</code>; a SQL Server LSN</th><td>accepted; accepted; 400; 400; 400; 400; 400</td><td>The version must be a non-negative 64-bit integer. Text positions are rejected.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>_update</code> with <code>version_type=external</code></th><td>400</td><td>Send the whole image as an index request.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">tie: <code>external</code> v100 over v100, then <code>external_gte</code></th><td>409, then accepted</td><td><code>external_gte</code> resolves a tie by arrival; the documentation says to use it with care.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The setting is documented as dynamic: <code>index.gc_deletes</code> is &ldquo;the length of time that a deleted
      document&rsquo;s version number remains available for further versioned operations. Defaults to 60s&rdquo;. The
      cost of a long window (the pages read do not document it) needs your own measurement. OpenSearch documents the same
      <code>version_type</code> values and a dynamic <code>index.gc_deletes</code> with a 60 s default; I did not run
      OpenSearch.
    </p>
    <h3>Three consequences</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The version has to be one integer.</strong> A PostgreSQL <code>lsn</code> in a Debezium event is already an
        integer (it fits while it stays below 2<sup>53</sup> in JavaScript, about 9 PB of WAL). A SQL Server LSN has three
        parts (for example <code>0000003a:00002f50:0005</code>) and a MySQL position is a file and an offset: you must
        pack them into one monotonic integer yourself, which is a design, not a feature, and positions from different log
        lineages are not comparable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A tie is silently dropped.</strong> If two changes to one key could share a version, plain
        <code>external</code> keeps the first and refuses the second. Debezium&rsquo;s PostgreSQL page does not say whether
        events in one transaction can share an <code>lsn</code>, so fold an ordinal into the integer or test your source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deletes need a plan.</strong> Either delete with the version and set
        <code>index.gc_deletes</code> above the longest replay you can suffer, or index a marker document
        (<code>deleted: true</code>) and filter it out of queries, purging it only once no replay can reach back that
        far. The first is what the page measured at 60 s by default; the second matches the
        <a href="{{ '/deletes-stay-deleted/' | url }}">deletes that stay deleted</a> lesson.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>A consumer sketch (request shapes run; the Debezium side untested)</h3>
<pre><code class="language-js">// Node 20+. Debezium JSON events with the PostgreSQL source block.
export function toBulkLines(events, { index, idField = "id", softDelete = false }) {
  const lines = [];
  for (const e of events) {
    if (e == null || e.op == null) continue; // a Kafka-style tombstone or a non-change record
    const row = e.op === "d" ? e.before : e.after;
    const version = e.source.lsn;
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(version) || version &lt; 0) {
      throw new Error(`position ${version} is not a safe non-negative integer`);
    }
    const meta = { _index: index, _id: String(row[idField]), version, version_type: "external" };
    if (e.op === "d" &amp;&amp; !softDelete) {
      lines.push(JSON.stringify({ delete: meta }));
    } else {
      lines.push(JSON.stringify({ index: meta }));
      lines.push(JSON.stringify({ ...row, deleted: e.op === "d" }));
    }
  }
  return lines.length ? lines.join("\n") + "\n" : "";
}

// Returns indexes to retry (429, 5xx) and indexes that were rejected (everything else).
export function classifyBulk(response) {
  const retry = [];
  const rejected = [];
  response.items.forEach((item, i) =&gt; {
    const r = Object.values(item)[0];
    if (r.status &lt; 300) return; // applied
    if (r.status === 409 &amp;&amp; r.error?.type === "version_conflict_engine_exception") return; // stale or duplicate
    if (r.status === 429 || r.status &gt;= 500) retry.push(i);
    else rejected.push(i);
  });
  return { retry, rejected };
}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Send the lines to <code>POST /_bulk</code> with <code>Content-Type: application/x-ndjson</code>. Commit the source
      offset only after every item is applied, a 409, or sent to a dead-letter store; a batch with a retry item is
      re-sent whole, which is safe because the write is idempotent. The two functions above were run against
      Elasticsearch 9.5.5 with hand-made events: a create and an update returned 201 and 200, a replayed older change and
      a duplicate returned 409 and 409, a delete returned 200, a late update after it returned 409, and the marker mode
      kept <code>{"id":2,"deleted":true}</code> at version 300 against a late update. No real Debezium event was
      used; this repository&rsquo;s unit test also runs them on hand-made events.
    </p>
    <h3>Redis as a cache</h3>
    <p>
      Two honest options. <strong>Invalidate, do not write:</strong> on every change event delete the cache key and let
      the reader reload from the database. A duplicate or a reordered event can only cause an extra miss. Debezium
      documents this pattern for Hibernate&rsquo;s second-level cache (an incubating Quarkus extension that evicts on
      update and delete). Race: a reader that loaded the old row before the change can still write it back after the
      eviction, which is why populating needs a guard. <strong>Guarded write:</strong> Redis has no compare-and-set on a
      numeric version, so use a script, which Redis documents as atomic. A plain <code>SET</code> has no guard: on Redis
      8.10.2, <code>SET b</code> then <code>SET a</code> left <code>a</code>.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-lua">-- KEYS[1] cache key; ARGV[1] version (log position, integer below 2^53);
-- ARGV[2] 'd' for delete; ARGV[3] document; ARGV[4] tombstone TTL in seconds.
-- Returns 1 when applied, 0 when stale or duplicate.
local current = redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], 'v')
if current and tonumber(current) &gt;= tonumber(ARGV[1]) then return 0 end
if ARGV[2] == 'd' then
  redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'v', ARGV[1], 'deleted', '1', 'doc', '')
  redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], tonumber(ARGV[4]))
else
  redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'v', ARGV[1], 'deleted', '0', 'doc', ARGV[3])
  redis.call('PERSIST', KEYS[1])
end
return 1</code></pre>
    <p>
      Run on Redis 8.10.2 with <code>redis-cli --eval</code>: v100 then v200 applied (1, 1); replay v100 and a duplicate
      v200 returned 0 and left v200 in place; a delete at v300 with a 3 s tombstone TTL made a late v200 return 0, and
      after the TTL expired the same late v200 returned 1 and the key came back. Redis lets you choose the tombstone TTL,
      so the window is yours, but it has the same shape as <code>gc_deletes</code>. Lua has one numeric type; the probe
      showed 2<sup>53</sup>+1 refused as &ldquo;not newer&rdquo; than 2<sup>53</sup>, so keep versions below 2<sup>53</sup>.
      Readers must treat <code>deleted = 1</code> as a miss.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="eos" aria-labelledby="eos-title">
    <h2 id="eos-title">Where exactly-once stops, again</h2>
    <p>
      None of these paths adds a shared transaction between the source and the destination. Debezium documents
      at-least-once delivery and no internal deduplication layer; the engine page tells you to expect duplicates after
      a crash; Datastream documents at-least-once; Zerobus tells you to deduplicate by source LSN. The achievable claim
      is exactly-once <em>processing</em>: a position-ordered idempotent write makes the duplicates harmless. See
      <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/#idempotency' | url }}">Exactly-once semantics</a> and
      <a href="{{ '/is-cdc-exactly-once/' | url }}">Is CDC exactly-once?</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try-it" aria-labelledby="try-it-title">
    <h2 id="try-it-title">Try it: reproduce the probe</h2>
    <p>
      With a throwaway Elasticsearch on <code>localhost:9200</code> (security off), this reproduces the central rows of the
      table. The commands match what was run, on port 19200 instead of 9200.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -s -XPUT 'localhost:9200/probe' -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"settings":{"number_of_replicas":0}}'
curl -s -XPUT 'localhost:9200/probe/_doc/7?version=100&amp;version_type=external' -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"a"}'
curl -s -XPUT 'localhost:9200/probe/_doc/7?version=200&amp;version_type=external' -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"b"}'
curl -s -XPUT 'localhost:9200/probe/_doc/7?version=100&amp;version_type=external' -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"a"}'   # expect 409
curl -s -XDELETE 'localhost:9200/probe/_doc/7?version=300&amp;version_type=external'
curl -s -XPUT 'localhost:9200/probe/_doc/7?version=200&amp;version_type=external' -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"b"}'   # 409 now, accepted after about 60 s</code></pre>
  </section>

